properties.rationale:
THE CAUSE (fact:root-cause-one-regex-cannot-separate-door-reads-because-the-read-set-is-not-in-the-command-and-not-served-2026-10-02), confirmed on main 293f957 before any change. The door's read/write classification (the served read_only_hint) lived inside the process, while a terminal's approval is decided on the command's text, which named only a tool. Nothing on the command line served the read set: `reflow2-mcp read` was an unrecognized subcommand, and find_tools items carry only tool, summary, parameters and score. Item 1 had since made `--read-only --call` refuse writers, but in its own words. It also refused export_graph's file only after opening the store, and logged an INFO line on every call and an rmcp WARN line beside each refusal.

WHY THE BINARY AND NOT THE WRAPPER. The classification is the binary's served surface, so a shell verb could only ask the binary for it. A wrapper-only verb would be missing wherever only the binary is installed. The wrapper already hands every word it does not own to the binary, so there is one implementation, tested through the real binary (and the pass-through is driven in tools/test_init.py).

WHY ONE RULE IS ENOUGH. Only the tool, its JSON and --graph-path (now a global flag) can follow a verb. Every other flag after it is refused by the parser, and any flag before it by the verb's row in the one-shot table. A walk of every --help flag typed after `read`, beside a read and beside a writer, pins this.

THE ANNOTATION CHECK the requirement asks for. Every read-annotated tool was run with schema-built arguments through `read` AND through plain `--call` (no --read-only) on a seeded design. Neither changed anything on disk beyond item 1's stated bookkeeping (RocksDB's own files, the usage ledger, the handshake record and the version stamp), nor the design's content hash. This was measured first on main 293f957 (64 of 78 answered, 14 refused their arguments; only the usage ledger moved) and is kept as a test.

LEFT OUT, deliberately:
- a shell redirection on an approved line is the shell writing, which reflow2 cannot see. SETUP.md says so, and quotes VS Code's own statement that auto-approval is best effort, not a security boundary;
- how VS Code treats a heredoc under autoApprove is not established;
- find_tools items are not marked read or write: the refusal names `write`, and `read --list` serves the set;
- the held-graph snapshot route is inherited from --call unchanged and not separately tested here.
